"Explaining these loops took a very long time. About 1,900 years ago, the astronomer Ptolemy put Earth at the center and moved each planet on small circles riding on bigger circles. His system worked well enough to predict the planets, but it was complicated. In 1543, Copernicus put the Sun at the center instead, and the loops suddenly made simple sense: they are just what you see when a moving Earth overtakes another planet. Retrograde motion became one of the strongest clues that Earth itself is moving.": "f304fc2c6adf",
"How did retrograde motion help show that Earth moves?": "3d4e3f153763",
"In Chinese, the five planets you can see with the naked eye are named after the five elements: Mercury is the water star, Venus the metal star, Mars the fire star, Jupiter the wood star, and Saturn the earth star. Their older names describe how they behave. Jupiter was the \"year star,\" because it moves about one twelfth of the way around the sky each year, and people used it to count years. Venus was called Qiming, the morning star, when it rose before dawn, and Changgeng, the evening star, when it shone after sunset.": "4c43c85b99d7",
"Keep the camera steady when you take the photo.": "11aac73a9ab2",
"So why does a planet sometimes go backward? Think of riding in a car on the highway. As you pass a slower truck, the truck seems to slide backward against the faraway mountains, even though it is still moving forward. Earth, on its faster inside track, passes Mars about every 26 months. For a couple of months around that time, Mars appears to drift west against the stars, then turns east again. Astronomers call this retrograde motion. It happens when the planet is on the opposite side of Earth from the Sun, so the planet is also at its closest, brightest, and up all night.": "f789b413e0ac",
"The ancient Greeks noticed that five of the brightest \"stars\" do not stay put. Night after night, they drift slowly through the constellations of the zodiac, and every so often one of them stops, turns around, and moves backward for weeks before going forward again. The Greeks called them planetes, which means \"wanderers,\" and that is where our word planet comes from. Chinese astronomers watched the same lights. They named Mars Yinghuo, \"the dazzling puzzler,\" because its path was so hard to predict.": "a039dd4187ec",
"The ancient Greeks noticed that five of the brightest \"stars\" do not stay put. Night after night, they drift slowly through the constellations of the zodiac, and every so often one of them stops, turns around, and moves backward for weeks before going forward again. The Greeks called them planetes, which means \"wanderers,\" and that is where our word planet comes from. Chinese astronomers watched the same lights. They named Mars Yinghuo, \"the dazzling puzzler,\" because its path was so hard to predict. Today we know that the planets, Earth included, all travel around the Sun in the same direction and in nearly the same flat plane. That is why they always appear near the zodiac, along the Sun's yearly path. The closer a planet is to the Sun, the faster it goes. Mercury makes a full lap in 88 days, Earth in a year, Mars in about two years, Jupiter in about 12 years, and Saturn in almost 30. Seen from Earth, a planet usually creeps a little farther east among the stars each night. So why does a planet sometimes go backward? Think of riding in a car on the highway. As you pass a slower truck, the truck seems to slide backward against the faraway mountains, even though it is still moving forward. Earth, on its faster inside track, passes Mars about every 26 months. For a couple of months around that time, Mars appears to drift west against the stars, then turns east again. Astronomers call this retrograde motion. It happens when the planet is on the opposite side of Earth from the Sun, so the planet is also at its closest, brightest, and up all night. Explaining these loops took a very long time. About 1,900 years ago, the astronomer Ptolemy put Earth at the center and moved each planet on small circles riding on bigger circles. His system worked well enough to predict the planets, but it was complicated. In 1543, Copernicus put the Sun at the center instead, and the loops suddenly made simple sense: they are just what you see when a moving Earth overtakes another planet. Retrograde motion became one of the strongest clues that Earth itself is moving. In Chinese, the five planets you can see with the naked eye are named after the five elements: Mercury is the water star, Venus the metal star, Mars the fire star, Jupiter the wood star, and Saturn the earth star. Their older names describe how they behave. Jupiter was the \"year star,\" because it moves about one twelfth of the way around the sky each year, and people used it to count years. Venus was called Qiming, the morning star, when it rose before dawn, and Changgeng, the evening star, when it shone after sunset. You do not need a telescope to find a planet. Look near the zodiac for a bright point that shines with a steady light, because stars twinkle more than planets do. Venus is the brightest thing in the night sky after the Moon, and Jupiter comes next. A planet will not stay among the same stars for long, so it never appears on the star maps from Lesson 5. Check the list of tonight's planets below, then look up and watch a wanderer move from one week to the next.": "743fa5711b87",
